    {
      "id": "qtm-m3-cumulative-distribution-c1",
      "heading": "What the CDF is",
      "explanation_md": "The **probability distribution** of a discrete random variable **lists the probabilities** of each value.\n\nThe **cumulative probability** is the probability that the random variable is **at most** some value x: the **sum of all the probabilities of the values i of X that are less than or equal to x**.\n\nThe **cumulative distribution function (CDF)**:\n\n**F(x) = P(X ≤ x) = Σ P(i) over all i ≤ x**\n\nThink of F(x) as a **running total**: start at the smallest value and keep adding probabilities until you reach x.",
      "example_ids": [],
      "key_terms": [
        {
          "term": "Cumulative probability",
          "meaning": "The probability that the random variable is at most some value x.",
          "source_ref": "qtm--module-3#31"
        },
        {
          "term": "Cumulative distribution function F(x)",
          "meaning": "F(x) = P(X ≤ x) = sum of P(i) over all i ≤ x.",
          "source_ref": "qtm--module-3#31"
        }
      ],
      "exam_line": "CDF: F(x) = P(X ≤ x) = Σ P(i) for all i ≤ x (probability that X is at most x).",
      "clarification": null,
      "check_questions": [
