    {
      "id": "qtm-m3-cumulative-distribution-c2",
      "heading": "Two dice with the F(X) column",
      "explanation_md": "Here is the two dice table with the running total F(X):\n\n| X | P(X) | F(X) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2 | 1/36 | 1/36 |\n| 3 | 2/36 | 3/36 |\n| 4 | 3/36 | 6/36 |\n| 5 | 4/36 | 10/36 |\n| 6 | 5/36 | 15/36 |\n| 7 | 6/36 | 21/36 |\n| 8 | 5/36 | 26/36 |\n| 9 | 4/36 | 30/36 |\n| 10 | 3/36 | 33/36 |\n| 11 | 2/36 | 35/36 |\n| 12 | 1/36 | 36/36 |\n\n(The slide's table prints \"30//36\" at X = 9; it means 30/36.)\n\n**Q2. P(sum at most 5) = P(X ≤ 5):**\n\nP(2) + P(3) + P(4) + P(5) = 1/36 + 2/36 + 3/36 + 4/36 = **10/36**. That is just F(5).\n\n**Q3 (\"greater than and equal to 9\").** The slide's working adds the top values:\n\nP(X > 9) = P(10) + P(11) + P(12) = 3/36 + 2/36 + 1/36 = **6/36 = 1/6**\n\nWatch out: that working does not include 9 itself. If you include 9 as the question words say, add P(9) = 4/36 to get 10/36, which is the same as 1 - F(8) = 1 - 26/36.\n\nThe slide notes these sums are **also known as cumulative probability distributions**.",
      "example_ids": [
        "qtm-ex-m3-two-dice-sum"
      ],
      "key_terms": [],
      "exam_line": "P(X ≤ 5) = 1/36 + 2/36 + 3/36 + 4/36 = 10/36 = F(5); the slide's P(X > 9) = 3/36 + 2/36 + 1/36 = 6/36 = 1/6.",
      "clarification": null,
