    {
      "id": "qtm-m3-cumulative-distribution-c3",
      "heading": "Using F for a range: the baby girl scenario",
      "explanation_md": "**Question:** in 4 babies being born, what is the probability of **1, 2 or 3 girls**?\n\nUse the CDF: everything up to 3, minus everything up to 0.\n\nP(1 ≤ X ≤ 3) = **F(3) - F(0) = 14/16**\n\nCheck with the bars: F(3) = 1/16 + 4/16 + 6/16 + 4/16 = 15/16 and F(0) = 1/16, so 15/16 - 1/16 = 14/16. Or add the three middle bars: 4/16 + 6/16 + 4/16 = 14/16.\n\nSo for a range, take F at the top of the range and subtract F at the value just below the range, exactly as the slide does with F(3) - F(0).",
      "example_ids": [
        "qtm-ex-m3-baby-girls-cdf"
      ],
      "key_terms": [],
      "exam_line": "P(1 ≤ X ≤ 3) = F(3) - F(0) = 14/16 for the number of girls in 4 births.",
      "clarification": null,
